{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2601.02950","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2601.02950","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2601.02950","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Furong","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Furong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roy","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Roy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengwei","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Hengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Monica","familyName":"Agrawal","name":"Agrawal, Monica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Batch-of-Thought: Cross-Instance Learning for Enhanced LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-01-06T11:47:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-07T01:39:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-07T00:18:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:20:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-10T03:57:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-12T01:05:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-11-06T19:49:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-11-10T01:17:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current Large Language Model reasoning systems process queries independently, discarding valuable cross-instance signals such as shared reasoning patterns and consistency constraints. We introduce Batch-of-Thought (BoT), a training-free method that processes related queries jointly to enable cross-instance learning. By performing comparative analysis across queries, BoT identifies high-quality reasoning templates, detects errors through consistency checks, and amortizes computational costs. We instantiate BoT within a multi-agent reflection architecture (BoT-R), where a Reflector performs joint evaluation to unlock mutual information gain unavailable in isolated processing. Experiments across three model families and six benchmarks demonstrate that BoT-R consistently improves accuracy and confidence calibration while reducing inference costs by up to 61%. Our theoretical and experimental analysis reveals when and why batch-aware reasoning benefits LLM systems. Our code is available at https://github.com/xuanyang19/BoT."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2026 Main Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.02950","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-01-07T02:45:12Z","registered":"2026-01-07T02:45:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-11-10T10:02:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2611.12098","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2611.12098","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2611.12098","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuaifan","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Shuaifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyang","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xinyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanyan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yanyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueyang","familyName":"Min","name":"Min, Yueyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingzhou","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yingzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongcheng","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zhongcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaopeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaopeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Universal Fault-Tolerant Quantum Computing Architecture with Globally Driven Atom Arrays"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-11-09T09:18:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-11-10T05:06:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-11","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Scalable quantum computation requires reconciling programmability with simple, high-fidelity control. Here, we propose a universal fault-tolerant architecture using globally driven dual-species atom arrays. In a hybrid neutral-atom system, interspecies Rydberg interactions enable qubit addressability without locally addressed quantum gates. We construct a universal native instruction set, Global Rydberg Atom Instruction Language (GRAIL), and show that crosstalk errors can be suppressed to the $10^{-4}$ level. We compile surface-code error correction and magic state cultivation into GRAIL, and parallelize the compilation to suppress crosstalk. Under a hardware-inferred circuit-level noise model, we obtain an error-correction threshold of $1.43\\%$ for the resulting quantum memory and a logical error rate below $10^{-9}$ for magic states cultivated at an experimentally accessible Rydberg Rabi frequency. By replacing local gate addressing with atom-mediated selectivity, this architecture removes the associated calibration requirements, offering a route to simpler control for large-scale fault-tolerant neutral-atom computation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Includes Supplementary Information"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12098","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-11-10T09:30:57Z","registered":"2026-11-10T09:30:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-11-10T09:30:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2611.11695","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2611.11695","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2611.11695","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhixiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ulas","familyName":"Bagci","name":"Bagci, Ulas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Language model capabilities fail in a shared, predictable order under compression and perturbation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-11-08T23:30:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-11-10T04:22:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-11","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Compression can preserve a language model's average accuracy while selectively degrading individual capabilities. Whether these losses follow method-specific patterns or a transferable order is unclear. We applied seven perturbation and compression methods to eight instruction-tuned models, scoring 16 tasks in four answer formats against preregistered predictions. Short-answer capabilities failed in nearly the same order under four random perturbations with different mechanisms (median correlation 0.90) and, within each model, under pruning and quantization (0.875). Intact answer margin, answer frequency and answer format predicted this order. Fragility profiles measured under random perturbation predicted which tasks and facts compression removes first and, after four-task calibration, held-out task accuracy (median absolute error 0.11-0.12, against 0.20-0.21 for uniform decline); profiles borrowed from other model families did almost as well. The order did not transfer across models under GPTQ quantization or hold for long-form tasks. Within short answers, what compression removes is predictable before deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"45 pages (34 main text with Methods and Extended Data, 11 Supplementary Information), 4 figures, 6 Extended Data figures. Code, protocols and data: https://github.com/ZhixiangWang-CN/damage-order-artifacts ; interactive summary: https://zhixiangwang-cn.github.io/damage-order-artifacts/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11695","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-11-10T09:20:34Z","registered":"2026-11-10T09:20:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-11-10T09:20:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2611.10063","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2611.10063","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2611.10063","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carson","familyName":"Rodrigues","name":"Rodrigues, Carson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Clive","familyName":"Rodrigues","name":"Rodrigues, Clive","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aravind Reddy","familyName":"G","name":"G, Aravind Reddy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Done Is Not Correct: Measuring Silent Failures and Self-Verification Calibration When LLM Agents Take CAD Actions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-11-06T09:05:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-11-10T01:08:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-11","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When a large language model (LLM) agent writes parametric CAD code from a natural-language specification, syntactic success decouples from semantic correctness. We release a benchmark and harness that measure this gap directly: 13 specification tasks whose every requirement maps to machine-checkable properties of the produced B-rep, measured by the kernel against published tolerance bands, with reference solutions and the full graded attempt corpus. The exact, self-audited oracle isolates two quantities no prior CAD benchmark reports: the silent-failure rate (a valid, renderable solid, an explicit completion claim, and at least one failed semantic check) and completion-claim calibration. Across four models from four vendors (156 attempts per architecture), single-shot agents claimed completion on 98% of attempts and were silently wrong on 14.1%; a tool-use loop that enforces measure-then-claim cut silent failures to 3.2% and raised claim precision from 64% to 96%, and a four-model ablation attributes the change to the claim contract rather than the feedback. A planner-coder-checker pipeline matched that precision at lower coverage with no success gain. Stated confidence was miscalibrated single-shot (ECE 0.32) and became informative under verification for three of four models. Hardening our own oracle flipped 18 of 519 regraded verdicts from pass to fail and none in reverse; a blind expert audit overturned 3 of the 18 silent failures outside the one ambiguously specified task; and fourfold scaling of every tolerance band leaves the contrast intact."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 3 figures, 2 tables. Benchmark, harness, per-attempt records and a 3D viewer: https://github.com/rodriguescarson/cad-silent-failure-bench (archived at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22312792)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10063","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-11-10T08:37:18Z","registered":"2026-11-10T08:37:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-11-10T08:37:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.5281/zenodo.24010068","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.5281/zenodo.24010068","identifiers":[],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["FAIR+D Canon™ Global Institute"],"givenName":"Bidyut","familyName":"Mazumdar","name":"Mazumdar, Bidyut","nameIdentifiers":[{"nameIdentifierScheme":"ORCID","nameIdentifier":"0009-0007-5615-3558"}]}],"titles":[{"title":"NEXUSCAP-X™ — Global Critical Infrastructure Capacity Intelligence, Access \u0026 Orchestration Platform  Proprietary Commercial Software IP | Enterprise Software \u0026 Integrated Engineering Release | Master Asset Preservation and Verification Bundle | Version 2.0.0"}],"publisher":"Zenodo","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"NEXUSCAP-X Critical Infrastructure Intelligence Grid Interconnection Readiness Infrastructure Capacity Intelligence Capacity Discovery Infrastructure Capacity Assessment Infrastructure Access Coordination Grid Flexibility Energy Infrastructure Digital Infrastructure Data Center Infrastructure Infrastructure Project Viability Enterprise Software Architecture Proprietary Commercial Technology IP Commercial Software Licensing Technology Transfer Infrastructure Decision Support Enterprise SaaS Evidence Management Infrastructure Planning"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-11-10","dateType":"Issued"},{"date":"2026-11-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"COPYRIGHT AND PROPRIETARY COMMERCIAL INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY NOTICE — VERSION 2.0  Copyright © 2026 Bidyut Mazumdar. All Rights Reserved.  NEXUSCAP-X™ — Global Critical Infrastructure Capacity Intelligence, Access \u0026 Orchestration Platform is identified as a proprietary commercial technology and intellectual property asset. It encompasses the associated original software, source code, system architecture, technical designs, methodologies, specifications, documentation, engineering materials, distinctive branding and other related intellectual property, to the extent owned or lawfully controlled by the rights holder.  Rights Holder: Bidyut Mazumdar ORCID: 0009-0007-5615-3558  1. Reservation of Rights  All rights in the NEXUSCAP-X™ materials that are owned or lawfully controlled by the rights holder are expressly reserved, subject to applicable law and any third-party rights or obligations.  Publication, deposit, DOI assignment, public accessibility or possession of this resource does not constitute an assignment, transfer, abandonment, waiver or unrestricted grant of intellectual property or commercial exploitation rights.  2. Commercial Use and Authorization  No commercial licence or unrestricted right to exploit the proprietary NEXUSCAP-X™ materials is granted merely by accessing, downloading or possessing this publication.  Unless expressly authorized through a separate written agreement executed by the rights holder, the following activities are not authorized to the extent they involve rights controlled by the rights holder:  - Commercial exploitation or commercial deployment. - Reproduction or redistribution beyond applicable legal permissions. - Modification or creation of derivative works. - Incorporation into third-party commercial products or services. - Enterprise, OEM or embedded technology utilization. - Sublicensing, assignment or onward transfer of proprietary rights. - Technology transfer, commercialization or royalty-bearing exploitation.  Any permitted evaluation or other use remains subject to the applicable written authorization and relevant third-party licence conditions.  3. Commercial Licensing and Technology Transfer  Commercial access, enterprise deployment, OEM integration, embedded licensing, sublicensing, technology transfer and royalty-bearing arrangements require separately negotiated and executed written authorization.  The scope, permitted uses, territorial coverage, duration, fees, royalties, sublicensing conditions, confidentiality obligations and other commercial terms must be expressly established in the applicable agreement.  4. Third-Party Materials and Independent Rights  Third-party software, libraries, dependencies, standards, trademarks, components and other externally controlled materials are not claimed as exclusively owned by the rights holder merely by their inclusion or reference in this resource.  Such materials remain subject to their respective copyright, licences, ownership conditions and applicable legal requirements. Nothing in this notice overrides or replaces those independent rights and obligations.  5. Intellectual Property and Legal Status Clarification  This notice identifies the declared rights holder and communicates the intended proprietary commercial rights position for the NEXUSCAP-X™ materials.  It does not, by itself, establish patent ownership, patent grant, trademark registration, statutory registration, freedom to operate, third-party rights clearance, independent certification or a judicial determination of ownership.  All rights and protections are subject to applicable intellectual property laws and the actual chain of title and ownership of the relevant materials.  Rights Holder: Bidyut Mazumdar ORCID: 0009-0007-5615-3558 Technology: NEXUSCAP-X™ Release: Version 2.0.0 Copyright Year: 2026  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED — PROPRIETARY COMMERCIAL SOFTWARE AND ENGINEERING INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY."}],"language":"en","types":{"schemaOrg":"SoftwareSourceCode","resourceTypeGeneral":"Software","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"COMP","resourceType":""},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"HasVersion","relatedIdentifier":"10.5281/zenodo.24010069","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"},{"relationType":"HasVersion","relatedIdentifier":"10.5281/zenodo.24098061","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2.0.0","rightsList":[{"rights":"NEXUSCAP-X™ Proprietary Commercial Intellectual Property License"},{"rightsUri":"http://rightsstatements.org/vocab/InC/1.0/","rights":"Copyright (C) 2026 Bidyut Mazumdar. All Rights Reserved."}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"NEXUSCAP-X™\n\nGlobal Critical Infrastructure Capacity Intelligence, Access \u0026 Orchestration Platform\n\nProprietary Commercial Software Intellectual PropertyEnterprise Software and Integrated Engineering ReleaseMaster Asset Preservation and Verification BundleRelease Version: 2.0.0Copyright Year: 2026Rights Holder: Bidyut MazumdarORCID: 0009-0007-5615-3558Resource Type: SoftwarePublisher: ZenodoVersion-specific DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.24098061Concept DOI for all versions: 10.5281/zenodo.24010068\n\n\n\n1. EXECUTIVE PUBLICATION STATEMENT\n\nNEXUSCAP-X™ (Global Critical Infrastructure Capacity Intelligence, Access \u0026 Orchestration Platform) is a proprietary commercial software and integrated engineering technology initiative focused on the structured management, assessment, organization and coordination of critical infrastructure capacity information.\n\nThe platform's development direction encompasses infrastructure capacity intelligence, project-readiness assessment, evidence management, infrastructure-related decision support, enterprise application architecture, access coordination, technical provenance and future sovereign deployment pathways.\n\nVersion 2.0.0 consolidates two complementary software and engineering releases into a single, cryptographically identified Master Asset Preservation and Verification Bundle.\n\nThe publication consists of:\n\n\n\nNEXUSCAP-X Enterprise Software v2.0.0 — the principal enterprise software and backend application foundation.\n\nNEXUSCAP-X Integrated Enterprise Engineering Release v2.0.0 — complementary platform, billing, metering, operating-system, firmware and edge-hardware engineering reference materials.\n\nNEXUSCAP-X v2.0.0 Master Asset Preservation Bundle — a consolidated outer archive preserving both original release archives alongside their inventories, provenance information, metadata and verification artifacts.\n\n\nThis release is presented as a proprietary commercial technology asset and engineering-stage software foundation, not as an academic research paper, scientific dataset, independently validated research framework or certified infrastructure-control system.\n\nThe publication supports controlled technical inspection, asset preservation, software evaluation, engineering due diligence, prospective commercial licensing discussions and future development planning.\n\n2. PUBLICATION IDENTITY AND VERSION CONTROL\n\n\n\n\nMetadata\nPublication value\n\n\n\n\nProduct / Technology\nNEXUSCAP-X™\n\n\nFull name\nGlobal Critical Infrastructure Capacity Intelligence, Access \u0026 Orchestration Platform\n\n\nRelease\n2.0.0\n\n\nRelease classification\nProprietary commercial software and integrated engineering asset\n\n\nResource type\nSoftware\n\n\nPublisher\nZenodo\n\n\nCreator\nBidyut Mazumdar\n\n\nORCID\n0009-0007-5615-3558\n\n\nCopyright\n© 2026 Bidyut Mazumdar, subject to applicable law and third-party rights\n\n\nVersion DOI\n10.5281/zenodo.24098061\n\n\nConcept DOI\n10.5281/zenodo.24010068\n\n\nPrimary publication artifact\nMaster Asset Preservation Bundle\n\n\nPrimary archive format\nZIP\n\n\nIntegrity algorithm\nSHA-256\n\n\n\n\nVersioning policy: Version 2.0.0 identifies this specific release. The version-specific DOI identifies the corresponding Zenodo record version, while the concept DOI identifies the record across its versions and resolves to the latest published version.\n\nThe DOI values above are the identifiers displayed in the Zenodo record preview. Their publication and registration status is governed by Zenodo's publication process.\n\n3. TECHNOLOGY OVERVIEW\n\nNEXUSCAP-X™ is designed as an information-management and decision-support technology foundation for organizing infrastructure-related capacity information, project requirements, supporting evidence, dependencies and readiness indicators.\n\nIts architectural direction addresses a complex infrastructure information environment in which project developers, engineering teams, infrastructure stakeholders and institutional decision-makers may need structured information about:\n\n\n\nInfrastructure capacity and availability indicators.\n\nGrid interconnection readiness.\n\nProject requirements and development dependencies.\n\nEvidence completeness and provenance.\n\nInfrastructure project screening.\n\nEnterprise project and tenant management.\n\nAccess coordination and decision-support workflows.\n\nTechnical auditability and information integrity.\n\nEnterprise integration and commercial entitlement management.\n\nFuture sovereign and edge-platform deployment.\n\n\nThe platform is intended to provide structured information workflows and technical foundations that can support infrastructure-related evaluation and planning.\n\nTechnical boundary: The included screening component is a deterministic, documented heuristic. It must not be interpreted as a validated electrical-grid simulation, power-flow solver, utility capacity determination, infrastructure dispatch mechanism or legally authorized grid-access decision system.\n\nNEXUSCAP-X does not replace professional engineering studies, grid operator assessments, regulatory approvals, utility interconnection procedures or decisions by legally authorized infrastructure operators.\n\n4. RELEASE COMPOSITION\n\nVersion 2.0.0 brings together two independently identifiable technical archives.\n\n4.1 NEXUSCAP-X Enterprise Software v2.0.0\n\nArchive: NEXUSCAP-X_Enterprise_v2.0.0.zipArchive size: 331,028 bytesArchive members: 70SHA-256:\n\nfcefc1146bdcd2e121822030e869b833057117a6860ed9b55e83c70f0e82c914\n\nThis release contains the principal enterprise backend software foundation and related technical materials.\n\nIts documented components include:\n\nA. Application and API Foundation\n\n\n\nPython 3.11+ application foundation.\n\nFastAPI backend and API endpoints.\n\nPydantic-based input validation.\n\nSQLAlchemy domain and persistence models.\n\nAlembic database migration materials.\n\nAPI and domain-level application components.\n\n\nB. Identity and Access-Control Foundations\n\n\n\nArgon2id password-hashing implementation.\n\nJWT-based authentication foundation.\n\nRole-based authorization mechanisms.\n\nTenant-scoped application records and query structures.\n\nAccess-control and security-related documentation.\n\n\nC. Evidence and Audit Architecture\n\n\n\nEvidence metadata registration.\n\nSHA-256-based integrity references.\n\nTenant-linked hash-chain audit-event recording.\n\nStructured project and evidence records.\n\nDocumented provenance and integrity mechanisms.\n\n\nD. Infrastructure Screening\n\n\n\nDeterministic capacity-evidence screening heuristic.\n\nStructured input validation.\n\nDocumented decision-support logic.\n\nMathematical and technical specifications.\n\n\nE. Engineering and Deployment Materials\n\n\n\nDocker and Docker Compose configuration.\n\nCI workflow configuration.\n\nPython distribution wheel.\n\nTechnical architecture documentation.\n\nSecurity and threat-model materials.\n\nOperational and deployment guidance.\n\nLaTeX mathematical source and compiled PDF.\n\nCommercial asset and intellectual-property documentation.\n\n\nThe release represents an engineering-stage backend foundation. It is not represented as a completed customer-facing enterprise SaaS service, production-certified platform or independently security-audited infrastructure product.\n\n4.2 NEXUSCAP-X Integrated Enterprise Engineering Release v2.0.0\n\nArchive: NEXUSCAP-X_Integrated_Enterprise_Engineering_v2.0.0.zipArchive size: 169,581 bytesArchive members: 44SHA-256:\n\n61d17bee417cf4882ec7a0bc890076c6ee76919bf504693df2e0b3c5cd89c975\n\nThis complementary engineering release extends the enterprise software foundation with reference implementations, integration materials and platform engineering specifications.\n\nA. Billing, Metering and Commercial Infrastructure\n\n\n\nBilling ledger reference implementation.\n\nInteger-minor-unit monetary accounting approach.\n\nUsage-metering primitives.\n\nTenant-scoped licensing and entitlement evaluation.\n\nEngineering-level billing and integration materials.\n\n\nThese components provide engineering foundations and reference patterns. They do not establish a fully operational production payment-processing service or a completed commercial billing deployment.\n\nB. Platform and API Engineering\n\n\n\nOpenAPI interface contract.\n\nMinimal integrated API service.\n\nPostgreSQL-oriented database schema.\n\nContainer deployment baseline.\n\nSaaS operations and integration documentation.\n\n\nC. Operating-System Architecture\n\n\n\nYocto/OpenEmbedded layer starter.\n\nLinux security-hardening configuration fragment.\n\nsystemd service-sandboxing configuration.\n\nSovereign edge-platform architecture materials.\n\n\nThe release does not claim to include a completed custom operating-system kernel or a production-signed operating-system image.\n\nD. Firmware Engineering\n\n\n\nC-language firmware update-policy interface.\n\nUpdate-policy header and implementation.\n\nHardware-abstraction integration requirements.\n\nSecure firmware-update development reference documentation.\n\n\nThe included materials do not establish production firmware certification, hardware-in-the-loop validation or a completed device-specific secure update deployment.\n\nE. Hardware and Edge Architecture\n\n\n\nEdge-appliance reference specification.\n\nHardware engineering requirements.\n\nDevice identity and update architecture.\n\nIntegration and production-readiness planning materials.\n\n\nThe publication does not include a claim of manufactured hardware, a completed physical appliance, provisioned HSM keys or independently validated hardware security.\n\nF. Mathematical and Technical Documentation\n\n\n\nIntegrated platform mathematical model.\n\nLaTeX source files.\n\nCompiled mathematical model PDF.\n\nEngineering and verification documentation.\n\nCycloneDX-format planning software bill of materials.\n\n\nThese materials provide a structured technical reference for further engineering and integration work.\n\n5. MASTER ASSET PRESERVATION BUNDLE\n\nPrimary publication artifact:\n\nNEXUSCAP-X_v2.0.0_Master_Asset_Preservation_Bundle.zip\n\nArchive size: 503,686 bytesArchive members: 16SHA-256:\n\n56872521a498bb211f9be7d7b01a1c3a6df2857a0c9c91e1292ea3ee0e4ed20b\n\nThe Master Asset Preservation Bundle is a separate outer ZIP archive containing the two original release archives and their associated preservation, inventory, metadata and verification materials.\n\nIts principal contents include:\n\n\n\nOriginal Enterprise Software v2.0.0 ZIP.\n\nOriginal Integrated Enterprise Engineering v2.0.0 ZIP.\n\nMaster archive index.\n\nEnterprise archive member inventory in JSON format.\n\nEnterprise archive member inventory in CSV format.\n\nEngineering archive member inventory in JSON format.\n\nEngineering archive member inventory in CSV format.\n\nOuter bundle payload index.\n\nBundle metadata.\n\nAsset register.\n\nProvenance record.\n\nCycloneDX-format bundle software bill of materials.\n\nSHA-256 checksum manifest.\n\nEight-pass preservation verification report.\n\nREADME.\n\nVerification procedures and supporting documentation.\n\n\nThe member inventories record archive paths, file sizes, compression information, CRC values and SHA-256 hashes of decompressed regular-file contents, as represented in the generated indexes.\n\nThe Master Bundle distinguishes original software archives from generated preservation and verification artifacts.\n\nOriginal-file preservation principle: The two source ZIP archives are embedded as their original binary contents. The Master Bundle is an additional preservation container; it does not replace, rebuild or modify either original archive.\n\n6. CRYPTOGRAPHIC IDENTIFICATION\n\nThe three principal artifacts are identified by the following SHA-256 values.\n\n\n\n\nArtifact\nSize\nSHA-256\n\n\n\n\nEnterprise Software v2.0.0\n331,028 bytes\nfcefc1146bdcd2e121822030e869b833057117a6860ed9b55e83c70f0e82c914\n\n\nIntegrated Enterprise Engineering v2.0.0\n169,581 bytes\n61d17bee417cf4882ec7a0bc890076c6ee76919bf504693df2e0b3c5cd89c975\n\n\nMaster Asset Preservation Bundle\n503,686 bytes\n56872521a498bb211f9be7d7b01a1c3a6df2857a0c9c91e1292ea3ee0e4ed20b\n\n\n\n\nSHA-256 is used to identify the exact byte-level contents of each archive. A matching digest provides strong evidence that a file is identical to the corresponding recorded artifact, subject to the integrity of the verification process and reference values.\n\nThe checksums are identifiers, not independent proof of authorship, legal ownership, security or technical correctness.\n\n7. EIGHT-PASS ARCHIVE PRESERVATION VERIFICATION\n\nThe Master Asset Preservation Bundle was subjected to eight defined archive-level verification gates.\n\n\n\n\nGate\nVerification activity\nReported result\n\n\n\n\nP1\nOriginal source archive SHA-256 identity verification\nPASS\n\n\nP2\nZIP readability and CRC integrity verification\nPASS\n\n\nP3\nDuplicate archive-member detection\nPASS\n\n\nP4\nUnsafe path and path-traversal screening\nPASS\n\n\nP5\nEncrypted-member, symbolic-link and special-file screening\nPASS\n\n\nP6\nIndividual member SHA-256 inventory generation\nPASS\n\n\nP7\nByte-for-byte preservation verification of both original ZIPs\nPASS\n\n\nP8\nFinal Master Bundle readback, CRC, internal checksum and embedded-original verification\nPASS\n\n\n\n\nReported archive-preservation result: 8 of 8 gates passed.\n\nThe reported archive checks establish the integrity and preservation properties tested by those procedures. They do not constitute an independent third-party security assessment, source-code audit, penetration test, software certification or production acceptance assessment.\n\n7.1 Software Test Evidence — Separate Verification Category\n\nThe release information supplied for this publication reports the following automated test outcomes:\n\n\n\nEnterprise Software release: 19 of 19 automated tests passed.\n\nIntegrated Enterprise Engineering release: 7 of 7 automated tests passed.\n\n\nThese figures are reported release-level test results. They should be interpreted in the context of their respective test suites, execution environments and recorded evidence.\n\nThey are not equivalent to independent verification of all software functionality, comprehensive security testing, production acceptance or certification.\n\nThe archive-preservation result and software-test results are separate evidence categories and must not be conflated.\n\n8. SECURITY ARCHITECTURE AND IMPLEMENTED FOUNDATIONS\n\nThe published materials document several security-related implementation foundations and engineering controls, including:\n\n\n\nPassword hashing using Argon2id.\n\nJWT-based authenticated API access.\n\nRole-based authorization foundations.\n\nTenant-scoped application data structures.\n\nPydantic input validation.\n\nSHA-256 integrity references.\n\nHash-linked audit-event recording.\n\nDeployment-hardening configuration materials.\n\nDocumented threat-model considerations.\n\nControlled application and API request handling.\n\n\nThese components demonstrate the presence of specified implementation materials. Their inclusion does not establish that the complete platform has passed independent security validation or is suitable for unrestricted production deployment.\n\n9. SECURITY, RELIABILITY AND PRODUCTION-READINESS BOUNDARIES\n\nThe v2.0.0 release is an engineering-stage software and integrated technology asset.\n\nThe following activities are not represented as completed or independently validated by this publication unless separately supported by additional evidence:\n\nSecurity Assurance\n\n\n\nIndependent penetration testing.\n\nIndependent security architecture assessment.\n\nComprehensive SAST and DAST assessment.\n\nIndependent dependency vulnerability assessment.\n\nFormal security certification.\n\n\nEnterprise Operations\n\n\n\nProduction PostgreSQL tenant-isolation validation.\n\nEnterprise SSO, MFA and SCIM implementation.\n\nImmutable audit-storage deployment.\n\nExternal signed audit checkpoints.\n\nProduction evidence-file custody and malware-scanning infrastructure.\n\nProduction SaaS operational acceptance.\n\n\nCommercial and Financial Operations\n\n\n\nProduction payment-provider integration.\n\nPCI DSS assessment.\n\nProduction subscription and payment operations.\n\nIndependent financial-control assessment.\n\n\nPlatform and Hardware\n\n\n\nProduction operating-system integration.\n\nProduction firmware integration.\n\nHardware-in-the-loop testing.\n\nPhysical appliance validation.\n\nHSM provisioning and production signing-key ceremonies.\n\nDisaster-recovery and backup-restoration exercises.\n\n\nInfrastructure and Institutional Validation\n\n\n\nUtility engineering validation.\n\nLive infrastructure integration.\n\nCustomer acceptance.\n\nRegulatory approval.\n\nIndependent operational certification.\n\n\nThese items represent outstanding or separately evidenced development, assurance and deployment requirements. Their inclusion in this section is intended to establish clear boundaries around the claims made for this release.\n\n10. COMMERCIAL INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY POSITION\n\nNEXUSCAP-X™ is presented as a proprietary commercial software and engineering intellectual property asset attributed to Bidyut Mazumdar, to the extent the relevant rights are owned or lawfully controlled by the rights holder.\n\nThe release includes software, technical architecture, engineering documentation, specifications, methodologies, reference implementations and associated commercial asset materials.\n\nIts commercial direction includes potential arrangements such as:\n\n\n\nControlled technical evaluation licensing.\n\nPaid enterprise pilot agreements.\n\nEnterprise software licensing.\n\nPrivate and sovereign deployment licensing.\n\nFuture SaaS subscription arrangements.\n\nOEM and embedded technology licensing.\n\nPlatform API licensing.\n\nTechnology-transfer agreements.\n\nFixed-fee and milestone-based commercial arrangements.\n\nNegotiated recurring or usage-based royalty structures.\n\n\nThese are prospective commercial pathways, not representations of executed agreements or existing commercial revenue.\n\nNo customer contract, royalty payment, commercial valuation, institutional partnership, patent grant, trademark registration or completed technology-transfer transaction is asserted by this publication.\n\nAny commercial exploitation, enterprise deployment, OEM incorporation, sublicensing, redistribution or technology transfer involving rights controlled by the rights holder requires separately negotiated written authorization, subject to applicable law and third-party rights.\n\n11. INTENDED USE AND PROSPECTIVE APPLICATION AREAS\n\nThe technology may be evaluated for potential use in:\n\n\n\nData-centre infrastructure project diligence.\n\nGrid-interconnection readiness workflows.\n\nIndustrial and infrastructure-development planning.\n\nInfrastructure capacity information management.\n\nEnterprise infrastructure project portfolios.\n\nEvidence and provenance management.\n\nInfrastructure-related decision-support workflows.\n\nPrivate and sovereign infrastructure information systems.\n\nFuture edge-platform integration.\n\nOEM and embedded technology assessment.\n\nEnterprise software licensing and technology-transfer evaluation.\n\n\nThese are prospective application areas. They do not establish existing customer deployments, utility approvals, infrastructure operator acceptance or independently validated operational outcomes.\n\n12. DEVELOPMENT AND COMMERCIALIZATION ROADMAP\n\nPotential future development areas include:\n\nA. Enterprise Product Development\n\n\n\nCustomer-facing enterprise dashboards.\n\nExpanded project and infrastructure evidence ontology.\n\nAdvanced project-readiness workflows.\n\nEnterprise identity federation and MFA.\n\nImproved tenant-isolation assurance.\n\n\nB. Infrastructure Data and Intelligence\n\n\n\nUtility and infrastructure data integrations.\n\nExpanded evidence ingestion and provenance workflows.\n\nScreening-model validation and calibration.\n\nAdditional decision-support capabilities.\n\nIntegration with authorized infrastructure information providers.\n\n\nC. Production Operations\n\n\n\nProduction SaaS infrastructure.\n\nResilient storage and backup architecture.\n\nOperational monitoring and incident management.\n\nProduction billing and subscription integration.\n\nIndependent security assessments.\n\n\nD. Sovereign and Edge Technology\n\n\n\nSovereign operating-system integration.\n\nDevice and firmware prototyping.\n\nSecure device identity and update processes.\n\nHardware integration and validation.\n\nOEM and embedded deployment engineering.\n\n\nEach future capability requires its own implementation, testing, validation, security assessment and release-acceptance evidence before it can be represented as operational or production-ready.\n\n13. PUBLICATION SCOPE AND TECHNICAL SIGNIFICANCE\n\nNEXUSCAP-X™ v2.0.0 provides a structured, version-identified software and engineering release accompanied by a consolidated preservation and verification artifact.\n\nThe three-artifact structure separates:\n\n\n\nThe original Enterprise Software release.\n\nThe original Integrated Enterprise Engineering release.\n\nThe consolidated Master Asset Preservation and Verification Bundle.\n\n\nThis separation provides a traceable basis for archive identification, preservation, inspection and comparison of the included release materials.\n\nThe publication establishes a reference point for the delivered software and engineering assets, their documented scope, associated inventories and recorded archive-integrity results.\n\nIt is intended to support controlled technical inspection, software evaluation, engineering due diligence, prospective commercial licensing discussions and future development planning.\n\nThe publication should be interpreted according to the actual contents of the deposited archives, the verification procedures described herein and the expressly stated technical and production-readiness boundaries.\n\n14. RIGHTS, LICENSING AND THIRD-PARTY MATERIALS\n\nCopyright © 2026 Bidyut Mazumdar. All Rights Reserved, to the extent the relevant materials are owned or lawfully controlled by the rights holder and subject to applicable law and third-party rights.\n\nNEXUSCAP-X™ is identified as a proprietary commercial technology asset.\n\nPublication, DOI assignment, repository accessibility, download or possession of the deposited materials does not, by itself, constitute:\n\n\n\nAssignment or transfer of intellectual property.\n\nAn unrestricted commercial licence.\n\nAuthorization for enterprise deployment.\n\nAuthorization for OEM or embedded incorporation.\n\nPermission for sublicensing or redistribution.\n\nAuthorization for technology transfer or royalty-bearing exploitation.\n\n\nCommercial access and permitted use must be established through the applicable written authorization or separately executed agreement.\n\nThird-party software, libraries, dependencies, standards, trademarks and externally controlled materials remain subject to their respective licences, ownership conditions and applicable legal requirements.\n\nNothing in this publication overrides third-party rights or creates rights that the rights holder does not own or control.\n\n15. DISCLAIMER AND RELIANCE LIMITATIONS\n\nThis publication is provided for technical documentation, controlled inspection, preservation, evaluation and prospective commercial technology assessment.\n\nIt does not constitute:\n\n\n\nEngineering certification.\n\nUtility or grid-operator approval.\n\nRegulatory approval.\n\nIndependent security certification.\n\nA guarantee of production readiness.\n\nA guarantee of commercial performance.\n\nInvestment, legal or financial advice.\n\nA representation of freedom to operate.\n\nA determination of ownership of third-party materials.\n\n\nProspective licensees, deployment organizations, infrastructure stakeholders and institutional evaluators should conduct appropriate technical, cybersecurity, legal, operational and commercial due diligence before relying on or deploying the technology.\n\nAll claims concerning implementation, testing, ownership, licensing and deployment must be interpreted within the evidence and limitations expressly documented in this release.\n\n16. CREATOR AND RIGHTS-HOLDER ATTRIBUTION\n\nCreator and declared rights holder: Bidyut MazumdarORCID: 0009-0007-5615-3558Technology: NEXUSCAP-X™Release: Version 2.0.0Copyright year: 2026Publication repository: Zenodo\n\nThe creator and rights-holder attribution identifies the person declared responsible for this publication and the associated proprietary rights position. It does not independently establish the legal chain of title for every included component or third-party material.\n\n17. KEYWORDS\n\nNEXUSCAP-X; Critical Infrastructure Intelligence; Infrastructure Capacity Intelligence; Infrastructure Capacity Management; Grid Interconnection Readiness; Capacity Evidence Management; Infrastructure Decision Support; Enterprise Software; Proprietary Commercial Software; Commercial Intellectual Property; Integrated Enterprise Engineering; Enterprise Architecture; FastAPI; Python; Multi-Tenant Architecture; Evidence Provenance; Audit Integrity; Billing Infrastructure; Usage Metering; Licensing Infrastructure; Firmware Security; Edge Computing; Sovereign Infrastructure; Yocto; OEM L